   147	## 1.5 State spine
   148	
   149	The per-slice flow below (§§ 2–11) is a state machine. Keep it
   150	honest by deriving position from the repo, not from memory:
   151	
   152	**State is a derived function of durable on-disk witnesses.
   153	`STATUS-<phase>.md` is a cache of that derivation, not the source of
   154	truth.** A slice's current state is the furthest state whose witness
   155	exists and verifies:
   156	
   157	| State              | Witness (what proves it on disk)                                              |
   158	| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   159	| `WORKTREE_CREATED` | `git worktree list` shows WT + branch at the chosen baseline                 |
   160	| `IMPLEMENTING`     | *(sole transient state — Agent task active; no durable witness)*             |
   161	| `IMPLEMENTED`      | `output.json` present at its path **and** branch head advanced past baseline |
   162	| `CHERRY_PICKED`    | equivalent commit verified on mainline (`git log --grep` / SHA)              |
   163	| `REVIEWED`         | `<phase>-review-<rts>.md` exists with a `## Verdict:` line                   |
   164	| `CLOSED`           | plan has `Phase <id> CLOSED` block + promoted verdict                        |
   165	| `CLEANED`          | `git worktree list` no longer shows the WT                                   |
   166	
   167	Each transition's **guard** = the prior state's witness exists and
   168	verifies; its **effect** = produce the next witness. §§ 5–11 are the
   169	transition bodies; § 9 is the transition function.
   170	
   171	Four invariants (the entire added formality):
   172	
   173	1. **Derived position.** State = furthest state whose witness exists
   174	   and verifies. On any conflict, **witnesses win over `STATUS`.**
   175	2. **Artifact-gated transitions, never belief-gated.** Advance only
   176	   when the prior witness is present. Never cherry-pick before
   177	   `output.json`; never spawn fix-N before the implementer's
   178	   completion event **and** its commits exist.
   179	3. **No undefined transition.** Any state with no satisfiable next
   180	   step — expected witness missing (failed implementer), a BLOCK
   181	   fix-N can't clear, fix-N past the § 6 round cap — **halts and
   182	   escalates** rather than improvising. Under a Steward-held loop the
   183	   halt goes to the **Steward**, who decides re-commission / re-scope /
   184	   escalate to HITL; with no Steward in the loop it goes straight to
   185	   the HITL.
   186	4. **Idempotent re-entry.** On resume, re-derive from witnesses and
   187	   continue at the first incomplete transition; a transition whose
   188	   witness already exists is a verified no-op.
