./dev/steward/steward-ledger.jsonl:104:{"ts": "2026-07-25T16:56:50.946Z", "seq": 104, "kind": "decision", "summary": "STANDING RULING (HITL 2026-07-25): the ORCHESTRATOR is the PREFERRED commissioning mechanism; /goal is NOT. Order: (1) /orchestrate (alias /orch) HITL-opened session = default; (2) Steward-spawned background orchestrator Agent (subagent_type=orchestrator) when the Steward holds the loop — returns once, does NOT notify on stall, needs an anti-stall directive + git polling; (3) /goal complete 0.8.z ONLY on explicit HITL instruction for that run, never the Steward's own choice. Rationale: /orchestrate carries the repo's versioned guardrails (§0 preflight, worktree rules, codex §9, canary-then-max-3); /goal runs to a PROSE condition judged from the transcript, but a 0.8.x ladder is GATED not autonomous (X0/AC sign-off, publish, landings outside a mandate must STOP the loop) — precedent seq-81 had to scope /goal to 'Slice 0 X0 ONLY (hard stop after)', fighting the command's nature. Landed in 4 docs so every future steward AND orchestrator sees it: STEWARD-HANDOFF §9 (full rationale) + §1/§2/§8 call-sites, .claude/agents/steward.md (durable role contract, hard rule + loop line), .claude/commands/orchestrate.md, .claude/agents/orchestrator.md. NOT a deprecation of /goal — it remains a real built-in and the ~6 doc references to it are not wrong.", "decider": "hitl", "refs": ["file:dev/plans/prompts/0.8.x-STEWARD-HANDOFF.md", "file:.claude/agents/steward.md", "seq:103"]}
./dev/steward/steward-ledger.jsonl:121:{"ts": "2026-07-27T16:55:06.823Z", "seq": 121, "kind": "decision", "summary": "SLICE 30 RUNS TO COMPLETION UNDER THE DISPUTED MECHANISM (HITL 2026-07-27): let it finish. It is mid-flight and green - fix-3 GREEN, closure output.json updated, branch tip c0090a48, last write under 2 minutes before this entry - and stopping a green in-flight slice to settle a process question would cost real work to prove a point that can be settled for future commissions instead. THIS RULES THE SMALL CALL ONLY. THE LARGER QUESTION IS EXPLICITLY NOT RULED AND STAYS OPEN. The conflict, verified verbatim by the Steward from both files: dev/design/orchestration.md line 39 says the Orchestrator is Main thread (you). Always; lines 47 and 48 say Do not spawn an orchestrator subagent, the main thread IS the orchestrator; line 483, section 10 hard rule 1, says Main thread orchestrates. No orchestrator subagent. AGAINST THAT, dev/plans/prompts/0.8.x-STEWARD-HANDOFF.md lines 240 to 243, section 9 mechanism 2, sanctions A Steward-spawned background orchestrator by name with subagent_type orchestrator and cites ledger precedent seq-51, seq-72 and seq-81. Both are governing documents and they flatly contradict each other. STEWARD ERROR SURFACED IN THE OPEN: I commissioned Slice 30 by mechanism 2 at seq-120 without catching the conflict. I followed my own role contract, which names the mechanism and carries three completed-release precedents, but the orchestration design of record forbids it in two places and I should have caught it before spawning; the section 3 cold-start points at orchestration.md sections 12.4 and 12.5, which I read, not sections 1 and 10, which I did not. Explanation, not excuse. THE ANTI-PATTERN IS NOT EMPTY: dev/learnings.md line 17 records its rationale as avoids nested-orchestrator confusion, main thread keeps plan plus verification in one context. Its citation feedback_orchestrator_thread.md is a MEMORY entry rather than a repo file, so the original evidence cannot be read from the repository. WHY THIS BLOCKS THE SENDMESSAGE WORK: the seat allowlist only bites a SPAWNED orchestrator, because a main thread holds the full tool pool - so whether .claude/agents/orchestrator.md line 4 should grant SendMessage is DOWNSTREAM of whether a spawned orchestrator is sanctioned at all. Settle the mechanism first and the grant follows. NO SEAT FILE, RULE DOC OR AGENT DEFINITION HAS BEEN CHANGED. Steward recommendation on the open question, held loosely: ratify mechanism 2 and correct orchestration.md sections 1 and 10, because the empirical record is three completed releases through it and the stated rationale concerns NESTED orchestrators, which is a different shape from a Steward holding a loop - but the Steward cannot read the original feedback that produced the rule and says so.", "decider": "hitl", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "30", "refs": ["git:23a436dc", "seq:120", "plan:dev/design/orchestration.md"]}
./dev/steward/steward-ledger.jsonl:122:{"ts": "2026-07-27T17:06:09.653Z", "seq": 122, "kind": "correction", "summary": "CORRECTION TO seq-121: THERE IS NO CONTRADICTION AND I WAS WRONG. seq-121 recorded a Steward error - that I commissioned Slice 30 by a mechanism dev/design/orchestration.md forbids. That framing is RETRACTED on the evidence. No guardrail was breached and there was no conflict to catch. THE DECISIVE FACT IS CHRONOLOGY, verified by me from git rather than from the subagent report. The anti-pattern lines - Do not spawn an orchestrator subagent at orchestration.md 47 to 48, and section 10 hard rule 1 at line 483 - both date to commit 72af7045 on 2026-05-17. The orchestrator and steward AGENT TYPES, the things that make subagent_type orchestrator expressible at all, were created in commit 31a73401 on 2026-07-02, FORTY-SIX DAYS LATER. A rule cannot prohibit a mechanism that was not constructible when the rule was written. Those sections are SILENT on a Steward-commissioned orchestrator, not prohibitive. SECOND INDEPENDENT LINE OF EVIDENCE, also verified by me: on 2026-07-25 commit 927ffb35 at 11:57:17 added section 9 mechanism 2 sanctioning the Steward-spawned orchestrator, and commit 427d2712 at 13:53:49 - the same author, the same day, under two hours later - edited orchestration.md at hunks starting line 91 and line 365 while leaving section 1 and section 10 rule 1 UNTOUCHED. Strong circumstantial evidence the two were never read as governing the same thing, though not proof of conscious consideration. THIRD: main thread in that doc is ROLE-INDEXED, not globally unique. Both seat files use parallel phrasing for two different sessions - orchestrator.md line 3 says The main thread plays this role, steward.md line 3 says The main thread of a slash-steward session plays this role - which would be self-contradictory if the phrase named one globally unique thread. FOURTH, SUBSTANCE OVER WORDING: count the orchestrators. A slash-orchestrate session is orchestrator then implementer, ONE orchestrator. A slash-steward session is steward then orchestrator then implementer, also ONE orchestrator, because the Steward is explicitly NOT an orchestrator per steward.md line 50. The stated rationale in dev/learnings.md line 17 is avoids NESTED-orchestrator confusion, and the cited B.1 incident of 2026-05-03 was a wrapper agent misreading spawn from main thread as instruction to spawn-again - a chaining failure at the leaf, whose adopted guard was removing Agent and Task from the IMPLEMENTER seat. The wording no orchestrator subagent over-reaches its own rationale, its incident and its guard, all three of which are about nesting. FIFTH: orchestration.md now positively ASSUMES a Steward above an orchestrator - line 95 to 98 routes a halt to the Steward under a Steward-held loop, line 399 to 404 says an orchestrator never escalates straight past the Steward, and section 12.5 at line 635 to 645 defines a concurrent seam-ownership contract splitting STATUS rows between orchestrator and Steward. A document forbidding their coexistence could not contain that contract. WHAT I GOT WRONG AND WHY: I read three lines and reported a conflict without dating them or asking what main thread denotes. The HITL caught it and directed the re-examination. The repo NEVER DEFINES main thread anywhere, and feedback_orchestrator_thread.md - the authority the anti-pattern cites - is a MEMORY entry not present in the repo, so the original rationale text cannot be read here; the under-definition is what made the misreading available and is itself the finding. CONSEQUENCE: the SendMessage seat grant is NO LONGER BLOCKED on a prior mechanism ruling, because mechanism 2 is sanctioned and uncontested.", "decider": "steward", "release": "0.8.20", "refs": ["seq:121", "git:72af7045", "git:31a73401", "git:927ffb35", "git:427d2712"]}
./dev/steward/steward-ledger.jsonl:123:{"ts": "2026-07-27T17:21:35.999Z", "seq": 123, "kind": "observation", "summary": "MECHANISM D IS NOT VIABLE - the claude --bg --agent orchestrator commissioning path is CLOSED, and the negative result is recorded so no future Steward re-investigates it. Commissioned to a claude-code-guide subagent against the HITLs three stated beliefs, then partly re-verified by me. (a) TUI VISIBILITY - belief CORRECT with nuance: a background session IS listed and monitorable via claude agents and the agent view, but you cannot reply to or peek at it from within an interactive session, which is the half that matters for steering. (b) MESSAGING - belief CORRECT IN EFFECT, but the subagents stated REASON is WRONG and I am recording the correction because the wrong reason would sink the option we are actually choosing. The subagent claimed SendMessage requires agent-teams membership with mailbox files under ~/.claude/teams/. I disproved that directly: that directory DOES NOT EXIST on this machine, and I nonetheless used SendMessage successfully THIS SESSION to resume an ordinary spawned subagent. The correct mechanism is that SendMessage addresses agents within the CURRENT SESSIONS agent graph; a CLI --bg session is a separate top-level session that is not in that graph, so there is no addressable peer. Same verdict, sounder basis. (c) BILLING - belief REFUTED. Background sessions draw on the same subscription entitlement as interactive ones and appear in the same usage accounting; there is no separate API metering for the --bg flag. Recorded because it removes a cost objection that would otherwise be carried forward as true. CONSEQUENCE: Option C is dead and Option B is the only package left standing. The corrected mechanism in (b) also strengthens B - parent-to-child messaging demonstrably WORKS in this session right now, so the earlier caveat that nobody had confirmed the seat grant would function is now partly discharged. PRECISION, NOT OVERCLAIM: my successful send was MAIN THREAD to SUBAGENT. The orchestrators failing case was SUBAGENT to ITS OWN CHILD, depth 1 to depth 2. Those are different edges and the second remains UNPROVEN; a throwaway depth-1 probe is still owed before relying on it. ALSO SURVEYED AND REJECTED: cloud routines give a main thread on subscription with observability but NO inter-agent messaging; experimental agent teams give messaging but the teammate is NOT a main thread and dies with the lead session; a second manual terminal requires the human, which is the constraint being removed.", "decider": "steward", "release": "0.8.20", "refs": ["seq:122"]}
