./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1716:  SEAT_SOURCE='the payload agent_type field'
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1749:    "a2c992b1d49d95b4a0d3157c12916be7911c1da1: feat(agent-seat-hardening): ASH-B GREEN — seat-path-guard PreToolUse hook, shipped UNWIRED",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1759:    "DESIGN FINDING THE STEWARD ASKED FOR — CAN THE HOOK DETERMINE WHICH AGENT/SEAT IS INVOKING IT? The honest answer is QUALIFIED YES-AND-NO, and the 'no' half is the important one. YES for a SPAWNED subagent: the PreToolUse payload carries `agent_type` (e.g. \"orchestrator\") alongside `agent_id`, so a spawned orchestrator/steward is positively identifiable with no cooperation from the agent and no configuration. NO for a MAIN-THREAD session: per the Claude Code v2.1.220 binary's own field documentation, `agent_id` is \"Absent for the main thread, even in --agent sessions\", and `agent_type` is present on the main thread ONLY for a session started with `--agent`. A `/orchestrate` or `/steward` session is an ORDINARY session running a slash command — no `--agent` flag — so its top-level thread carries NEITHER field. From the payload alone such a session is byte-for-byte indistinguishable from any other session in this repo, including a human's ad-hoc one. There is no way to close that gap from inside a hook: the information simply is not sent. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE SEAT MODEL: the hook mechanically guards the spawned case and can only OPT-IN guard the main-thread case, via the $FATHOMDB_SEAT environment variable set at session LAUNCH. Since dev/design/orchestration.md § 1.1 establishes that 'main thread' is role-indexed and that a /steward session's top-level thread IS the Steward, the usual Steward seat is exactly the unguarded case unless the launcher sets the env var. That is a real hole in the guard, not a rounding error, and it is stated in the hook header rather than papered over.",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1760:    "SECONDARY FINDING — WHY $FATHOMDB_SEAT OUTRANKING THE PAYLOAD IS NOT AN EVASION HATCH. The resolution order is env, then agent_type, then UNKNOWN. It has to be env-first or a main-thread seat could never opt in. That ordering is safe because an agent cannot set the variable for its own hook invocations: each Bash tool call is a separate process, `export` does not persist between them, and the hook is spawned by the harness with the SESSION's environment, not by the agent's shell. The env var is therefore a launcher-side control, not an agent-side one. Documented in the hook header and covered by test arms 38 and 39 (precedence proven in both directions).",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1761:    "Created .claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh — a PreToolUse hook implementing the dev/design/orchestration.md § 1.2 write-path boundary. Guarded seats: orchestrator, steward (matched case-insensitively, whitespace-trimmed). Denied: any path with a `src`, `engine`, or `tests` SEGMENT, plus test-source basenames (test_*.{py,sh,rs,ts,js}, *_test.{py,sh,rs,go,ts,js}, *.test.{ts,tsx,js,mjs}, *.spec.{ts,tsx,js,mjs}, conftest.py). Matching is segment-based on the NORMALIZED target (`.`/`..` resolved, surrounding quotes stripped), so it is identical for absolute paths, repo-relative paths, and paths inside ANY worktree — a guarded seat must not write source anywhere, not only under $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR.",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1781:      "resolution": "Three-part construction. (a) The detector is proven against a POSITIVE fixture first: a synthetic settings.json that DOES wire the hook must be detected, or the arm fails. (b) Candidate settings files are resolved from every place one can really live: $REPO_ROOT/.claude/, the PRIMARY CHECKOUT's .claude/ (reached via `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` + dirname, since a linked worktree has none of its own), and $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/ — settings.json and settings.local.json in each, deduplicated. (c) The proven detector is applied to each file that exists. In this worktree it really did reach and check /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb/.claude/settings.json and settings.local.json, and both are clean. If NO settings file exists anywhere (fresh clone / CI) the arm passes with an explicit loud message saying the invariant holds vacuously and pointing at the proven detector, so the pass is never mistaken for a positive observation."
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1786:    "ACCEPTED OVER-BLOCK, documented in the header: because segment matching runs over the WHOLE normalized path, a path whose ancestor directory is literally named src/engine/tests OUTSIDE this repo (the common `~/src/notes.md` shape) is denied for a guarded seat. Deliberate: guarded seats work inside the repo, over-blocking is the safe direction for a guard whose purpose is stopping source writes, and the deny reason names the path so a human can see instantly what happened. The alternative (anchoring to $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR) would have broken the brief's requirement that the guard work for paths inside ANY worktree.",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1789:    "The suite grew from the brief's 16 required arms to 55 assertions across 43 numbered arms. All 16 required arms are present with their required verdicts. The additions are: the four hook-shape assertions (exists / executable / bash shebang / fail-open EXIT trap present); one Bash write-verb arm each for tee, cp, mv, rm, dd of=, git checkout --, git restore, inline python3 -c, inline node -e, truncate; absolute-path-in-another-worktree; `..` traversal laundering; four extra false-positive guards; unhandled-tool; non-guarded agent_type; both directions of env-vs-payload precedence; the spawned-subagent shape (agent_id + agent_type together); case/whitespace tolerance; and two stderr-silence arms. Three malformed-input sub-arms were added to arm 12 (JSON with no tool_input, valid JSON of the wrong shape, empty stdin).",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:1797:  "next_step_for_orchestrator": "FIX-1 IS COMPLETE — re-run the codex § 9 review against agent-seat-hardening at 0ad90cc6 (fix-1 round 1 of the 3-same-finding / 6-total cap; the incremental diff vs the reviewed d31430ce is TWO files, .claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh and scripts/tests/test_seat_path_guard.sh, plus this output.json in the following docs commit). BOTH findings are closed and both were WIDER OR NARROWER THAN REPORTED IN WAYS THE REVIEWER SHOULD SEE. (i) Finding 1's literal witness does not reproduce — the `#` delimiters keep `src` from ever being a bare path segment — but the mechanism is real; four reproducing witnesses were found, observed RED, and are now arms 47-50. (ii) Finding 2 is wider: the SPACED `>| FILE` slipped too, because the `|`-to-newline rewrite runs before tokenizing, so the review's 'the code catches the spaced form' is not accurate and arm 54 was red as well. (iii) Fixing arm 50 required a second, unreported layer — the whitespace tokenizer shreds a quoted program word — which is the one piece of NET-NEW logic in this fix (absorb_quoted_word) and the piece most worth adversarial reading. NOTHING ELSE CHANGED: the hook is still UNWIRED (.claude/settings.json untouched and untracked, arm 16 green), and the four items flagged for the reviewer in the ORIGINAL next_step below — the .gitignore hunk (B-ASH-B-1), the deny-list-not-allow-list narrowing, the MultiEdit/NotebookEdit gap, and the main-thread identification hole — are ALL STILL OPEN AND STILL NEED A DECISION; fix-1 addressed neither of them and was not scoped to. Original next-step text follows. Run the codex § 9 read-only review against branch agent-seat-hardening at a2c992b1 (diff vs 822c6444: 4 files — .claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh new, .gitignore, scripts/agent-test.sh, scripts/tests/test_seat_path_guard.sh — plus this output.json in a following docs commit), then cherry-pick per convention. FOUR THINGS WORTH THE REVIEWER'S EYE, in priority order. (1) BLOCKER B-ASH-B-1, the .gitignore hunk: .claude/hooks/ is gitignored, so without it the hook is untrackable, un-cherry-pickable and absent in CI. I took the narrowest fix (un-ignore exactly one file by name). ACCEPT IT, or direct a move of the hook to scripts/hooks/ and drop the hunk — either is fine, but one of the two is required or the slice delivers nothing to mainline. (2) The deny-list-not-allow-list design decision: the hook enforces § 1.2's MUST-NEVER column only and never checks that a write falls inside the MAY column. Deliberate (an allow-list would block README/workflow/Cargo.toml edits and get the guard switched off), but it is a real narrowing of § 1.2 and deserves an explicit accept. (3) The MultiEdit/NotebookEdit gap: the brief scoped the hook to Edit, Write and Bash, so I implemented exactly that and named the gap loudly rather than silently widening scope. MultiEdit carries file_path and is a one-line addition if you want it closed now. (4) The main-thread identification hole recorded at the top of findings_addressed — the Steward's usual seat is a main-thread session and is therefore UNGUARDED unless $FATHOMDB_SEAT is set at launch. That is a Steward-level design decision, not something a hook can fix. STILL UNWIRED AND STILL HITL-GATED, NOT PART OF ASH-B: .claude/settings.json is untouched, so nothing about this branch changes the behaviour of any running session, including the orchestrator live in another worktree. Wiring is Phase 2 and will turn suite arm 16 RED — by design; that red arm is the review gate that forces wiring to be a conscious act. When Phase 2 happens, decide deliberately between project-global settings.json wiring (fires for the main thread AND every subagent in the repo) and agent-frontmatter hooks scoped to one component's lifetime (the v2.1.220 binary contains the strings 'Skipping frontmatter hooks for' and 'frontmatter hook(s) from', so that path exists) — and remember that the frontmatter path cannot help a main-thread seat, which is exactly the case $FATHOMDB_SEAT exists for."
