./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-retry-20260727T183445Z.log:5184:    "a2c992b1d49d95b4a0d3157c12916be7911c1da1: feat(agent-seat-hardening): ASH-B GREEN — seat-path-guard PreToolUse hook, shipped UNWIRED",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-retry-20260727T183445Z.log:5204:    "DESIGN FINDING THE STEWARD ASKED FOR — CAN THE HOOK DETERMINE WHICH AGENT/SEAT IS INVOKING IT? The honest answer is QUALIFIED YES-AND-NO, and the 'no' half is the important one. YES for a SPAWNED subagent: the PreToolUse payload carries `agent_type` (e.g. \"orchestrator\") alongside `agent_id`, so a spawned orchestrator/steward is positively identifiable with no cooperation from the agent and no configuration. NO for a MAIN-THREAD session: per the Claude Code v2.1.220 binary's own field documentation, `agent_id` is \"Absent for the main thread, even in --agent sessions\", and `agent_type` is present on the main thread ONLY for a session started with `--agent`. A `/orchestrate` or `/steward` session is an ORDINARY session running a slash command — no `--agent` flag — so its top-level thread carries NEITHER field. From the payload alone such a session is byte-for-byte indistinguishable from any other session in this repo, including a human's ad-hoc one. There is no way to close that gap from inside a hook: the information simply is not sent. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE SEAT MODEL: the hook mechanically guards the spawned case and can only OPT-IN guard the main-thread case, via the $FATHOMDB_SEAT environment variable set at session LAUNCH. Since dev/design/orchestration.md § 1.1 establishes that 'main thread' is role-indexed and that a /steward session's top-level thread IS the Steward, the usual Steward seat is exactly the unguarded case unless the launcher sets the env var. That is a real hole in the guard, not a rounding error, and it is stated in the hook header rather than papered over.",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-retry-20260727T183445Z.log:5206:    "SECONDARY FINDING — WHY $FATHOMDB_SEAT OUTRANKING THE PAYLOAD IS NOT AN EVASION HATCH. The resolution order is env, then agent_type, then UNKNOWN. It has to be env-first or a main-thread seat could never opt in. That ordering is safe because an agent cannot set the variable for its own hook invocations: each Bash tool call is a separate process, `export` does not persist between them, and the hook is spawned by the harness with the SESSION's environment, not by the agent's shell. The env var is therefore a launcher-side control, not an agent-side one. Documented in the hook header and covered by test arms 38 and 39 (precedence proven in both directions).",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-retry-20260727T183445Z.log:5208:    "Created .claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh — a PreToolUse hook implementing the dev/design/orchestration.md § 1.2 write-path boundary. Guarded seats: orchestrator, steward (matched case-insensitively, whitespace-trimmed). Denied: any path with a `src`, `engine`, or `tests` SEGMENT, plus test-source basenames (test_*.{py,sh,rs,ts,js}, *_test.{py,sh,rs,go,ts,js}, *.test.{ts,tsx,js,mjs}, *.spec.{ts,tsx,js,mjs}, conftest.py). Matching is segment-based on the NORMALIZED target (`.`/`..` resolved, surrounding quotes stripped), so it is identical for absolute paths, repo-relative paths, and paths inside ANY worktree — a guarded seat must not write source anywhere, not only under $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR.",
./dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-15-steward-positions-verify-20260721T041028Z.log:8150:dev/steward/tooling-port-reconciliation.md:43:| `orchestrator-guard.sh` (blanket on-`main` source-edit block) | **Skip** | The active `wake guard-check` PreToolUse hook (wired in `.claude/settings.json` on `Edit` + `Write`) already covers recorded on-`main` edit constraints. Verified present in `.claude/settings.json`. |
